**Random test generators** (often abbreviated RTG or ISG[[:0-1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Random_test_generator#cite_note-:0-1) for Instruction Stream Generator or Instruction Sequence Generator[[:0-1]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Random_test_generator#cite_note-:0-1)) are a type of [computer software](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_software "Computer software") that is used in [functional verification](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Functional_verification "Functional verification") of [microprocessors](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microprocessor "Microprocessor"). Their primary use lies in providing input stimulus to a [device under test](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Device_under_test "Device under test").\n\nIn a [simulation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logic_simulation "Logic simulation")/[testbench](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Test_bench "Test bench") verification environment, the simulator processes input created by the RTG and coverage monitors may be used to verify that the generator is properly testing the design.[[:2]](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Random_test_generator#cite_note-2)\n\nRandom test generators range in scope from simple [scripts](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scripting_language "Scripting language") and parameterized [macros](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macro_(computer_science\) "Macro (computer science)") that can be created in a matter of weeks to full featured systems requiring extensive software development. Random test generators are most often created by the designing organizations.